{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Three times a year.</b> Lest you say: concerning Shavuot and Sukkot, where it is written 'and you shall rejoice, you and your son and your daughter' etc., a person is obligated to bring his son up with him for the pilgrimage — but on the festival of Matzot, where 'rejoicing' is not written, the father is not obligated — therefore it says <b>three times a year shall all your males appear.</b> Another interpretation: <b>three times</b> — Scripture restores it for the sake of <b>and they shall not appear before HaShem empty-handed</b>, since above, 'shall not appear' was stated only regarding the festival of Matzot." "<b>All your males.</b> Everyone who is able to go up with <b>all your males</b> — excluded are those whose labor is degrading and repulsive and whose odor is foul, and who cannot go up with their fellows, for they are exempt, as our Rabbis expounded in the first chapter of Chagigah." "<b>At the festival of Matzot and at the festival of Shavuot.</b> It draws an analogy between the festival of Shavuot and the festival of Matzot: just as the festival of Matzot has make-up offerings all seven days, so too the festival of Shavuot." "<b>And at the festival of Sukkot.</b> It comes to teach us that it is last, or that it is the cause of the matter of 'do not delay.' <b>The order of the pericope Re'eh is complete.</b>"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:53:10.345564443Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}